  [1mPersistence mechanics.[0m The ring buffer stays the in-memory working copy; persistence is a mirror of it, not a second
  source of truth. On load, the browser adapter rehydrates the buffer from storage, so [38;5;153mexport()[39m returns pre-reload and
  post-reload records as one ordered list. Writing on every log call would serialize the whole buffer on every line, so
  instead it writes on a short debounce (~250ms) and unconditionally on [38;5;153mpagehide[39m, which is the event that actually
  survives reloads and tab closes. Storage is capped well under the ~5MB quota, and a quota error drops the oldest chunk
  rather than throwing — a logger that breaks the app it's instrumenting is worse than a logger that loses old lines.

  [1mThe fork I need you to settle:[0m [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m vs [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m. This one is not cheap to reverse in the way the sink is
  — it decides how long login-flow data sits on the user's disk, and changing it later doesn't clean up what the old
  choice already wrote.

  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — survives reload and in-tab navigation; cleared when the tab closes. Buys exactly the persistence
    you asked for, with the exposure window bounded to one tab session. Doesn't survive a browser restart or a crash
    that kills the tab.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives reload, tab close, browser restart, indefinitely until something clears it. Buys the ability
    to debug "it broke yesterday, here's my export." Costs: redacted-but-still-real user activity accumulating on a
    shared or public machine, and it needs its own expiry policy or it grows forever.

  [1mMy recommendation: [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m.[0m It satisfies "persist across a page reload" precisely, and on a login page the
  difference is whether the previous user's session history is still sitting there for the next person at that machine.
  If you want next-day debugging I'd rather add an explicit, user-initiated "save this export" than leave it
  accumulating by default.
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[38;5;16m[48;5;153m ☐ Storage [39m[49m

[1m[38;5;231mWhich browser storage should back the persisted buffer?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153msessionStorage (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mSurvives reload and navigation; cleared when the tab closes. Bounds how long login-flow data sits on the machine.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m localStorage
     [38;5;246mSurvives browser restart too, enabling next-day debugging. Accumulates indefinitely and needs its own expiry [39m
     [38;5;246mpolicy.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m localStorage with a TTL
     [38;5;246mSurvives restart but records older than a set age (e.g. 24h) are discarded on load. Middle ground, slightly more [39m
     [38;5;246mlogic.[39m
  [38;5;246m4. Type something.[39m
